The user just made an edit to the CSS file in the primary session. They changed:

```css
body {
  overflow: auto;
  height: auto;
}
```

to:

```css
body {
  overflow-y: auto;
  overflow-x: hidden;
  height: auto;
}
```

This is a refinement to prevent horizontal scrolling on the body element. The change from `overflow: auto` to `overflow-y: auto` and `overflow-x: hidden` ensures that:
- Vertical scrolling is allowed (auto)
- Horizontal scrolling is completely prevented (hidden)

This is likely in response to noticing that with the wider sidenote spacing, there might be some horizontal overflow issues. The `overflow-x: hidden` will clip any content that extends beyond the viewport width horizontally.

Since this just happened and I'm seeing it as an observation, I should acknowledge this change and explain what it does. The user is actively working on refining the layout.

The user just made another edit, this time removing the `overflow-x: clip;` line from the `.container.content` rule within the `@media (min-width: 48em)` block.

So they went from:
```css
/* Remove the independent content scrollbar; clip only horizontal overflow */
.container.content {
  overflow-y: visible;
  overflow-x: clip;
}
```

to:
```css
/* Remove the independent content scrollbar */
.container.content {
  overflow-y: visible;
}
```

This makes sense - they already added `overflow-x: hidden` to the body element, so they don't need `overflow-x: clip` on the container. By removing it, the horizontal overflow control is handled at the body level, which is cleaner.

The user is fine-tuning the overflow behavior, consolidating the horizontal overflow control to just the body element rather than having it in multiple places.
